#!/bin/sh
# koti-help — the manual, on the machine.
#
# ⛔ WHY THIS EXISTS RATHER THAN JUST docs/MANUAL.md: koti cannot fetch it.
# A machine that cannot fetch anything needs its documentation locally, or the
# documentation is only useful to someone sitting at a different computer. This
# is the short form; docs/MANUAL.md is the long one and they are kept in step
# by hand -- if you add a section there, add its heading here.
#
# ⭐ SECTIONS ARE ADDRESSABLE NOW (PLAN item 24). `koti-help STORAGE` prints one
# section, unpaged; `koti-help -l` lists them. That is what makes retrieval
# possible at all -- `koti help "how do I save a file"` is a keyword table in
# /usr/bin/koti that resolves to a section name and calls this. No model, no
# weights, and it cannot invent an answer, because every answer it can give is
# a section somebody wrote.
#
# 🪤 A NAMED SECTION IS NOT PAGED, DELIBERATELY. `more` waits for a keypress at
# the end, and on the serial console that reads as a hung machine (it cost a
# session on 2026-08-12 via koti-status). Whole-manual output still pages,
# because 90 lines on an 80x60 screen genuinely needs it.
#
# ⚠️ HEADINGS ARE PARSED, NOT LISTED: a heading is a line starting at column 0
# in capitals. Adding a section needs no table here -- but indenting one, or
# writing it in lower case, silently removes it from `-l` and from every
# `koti help` question that would have found it.
#
# Copyright (c) 2026 Joonatan Alanampa
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

manual() {
cat <<'EOF'
koti - a computer built from the transistor up.
RV32IMA + Zicsr, sv32 MMU, 32 MB SDRAM, Linux 6.12, on an ULX3S 85F at 25 MHz.

WHAT IS HERE
  Userspace is busybox on musl, statically linked: about 280 commands. The
  usual ones behave the way you expect -- ls cd cp mv rm mkdir cat less more
  grep sed awk find head tail sort wc du df mount umount ps top kill free
  uname dmesg date vi.

  vi is the editor. `less` and `more` page. `tree` is here. `bc` and `dc` do
  arithmetic. `tar`, `gzip`, `xz`, `unzip` handle archives.

  koti's own commands: `koti-help` (this), `koti-status` (what the machine is
  doing), `koti-net` (the internet, through the ESP32), and `koti` -- which
  has `koti peek` for looking at the hardware and `koti help` for finding the
  right section of this manual by asking a question.

STORAGE
  /mnt         the microSD's second partition, ext2, MOUNTED FOR YOU AT BOOT.
               Write here and it survives a power cycle.
  /            RAM (initramfs), unless the boot log said it switched to the
               card. ANYTHING WRITTEN HERE IS LOST AT POWER-OFF.

  The boot log says which happened: `koti: microSD mounted at /mnt`, or
  `koti: the microSD IS /` if the card is the root filesystem, or a line
  naming the reason it is neither. `mount | grep kotisd` confirms it.

  ⚠️ ext2 has NO JOURNAL. `sync` before pulling the power or you will lose
  whatever was still in the page cache. The card is checked with e2fsck at
  every boot, which repairs the ordinary damage and tells you about the rest.

CLOCK
  koti keeps time on a DS3231 module plugged into the J1 header, on its own
  coin cell. The kernel reads it before userspace starts, so `date` is right
  at the login prompt with nothing run and nothing configured.

    hwclock -r        what the chip says
    hwclock -w        copy the system time INTO the chip (do this once, after
                      setting the date, and it survives being unplugged)
    hwclock -s        copy the chip's time into the system clock
    i2cdetect -y 0    the bus. 0x68 is the clock; 0x57, if present, is an
                      EEPROM on the same little board that nothing uses.

  If `date` is wrong, set it and write it down:

    date -s "2026-08-14 21:30:00" && hwclock -w

  Two older mechanisms are still here and still work, and they are what runs
  if the module is unplugged:

    koti-net time     asks a web server what time it is, from the Date header
                      every HTTP reply already carries. Accurate to a second
                      or two, and it saves the answer to the card.

  At the next boot that saved time is restored only if it is LATER than what
  the clock already says — so with the RTC fitted it never fires, and without
  it the machine starts from when it was LAST RUNNING rather than from 1970.
  That fallback is monotonic, not correct: after a week unplugged it believes
  it is still last Tuesday.

  If `date` says 1970 with the module fitted, the chip is not being seen:
  check `i2cdetect -y 0` for 0x68, then `dmesg | grep -i rtc`.

  The DS3231 also measures its own temperature — that is how it stays accurate
  to a couple of seconds a month — and koti reads it out:

    cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0/temp1_input     millidegrees C

THE TWO CONSOLES
  The monitor (tty1) is the one you type at. The serial line (hvc0) is a
  MIRROR: it receives the same keystrokes, so a single login logs you in on
  both and every command runs twice. That is deliberate -- the serial echo is
  how the machine is debugged -- but it is why you may see doubled output.

KEYBOARD
  Finnish layout, loaded at boot (`koti: fi keymap loaded`). AltGr reaches
  @ $ { } [ ] \ | -- all of which a shell needs.

INTERNET
  koti has no address of its own -- no MAC, no PHY -- so ip, ping and wget
  cannot work. What it has instead is a modem: the ESP32 next to the FPGA,
  reached over /dev/ttyKOTI0.
    koti-net wake                     release the ESP32 from reset
    koti-net join <ssid> <password>   prints the address it was given
    koti-net scan                     the networks it can actually see
    koti-net get http://ADDR/ [HOST]  the page, on standard output
    koti-net time                     set the clock from a server
    koti-net off                      put it back into reset
  The address belongs to the ESP32, not to koti. http only, no https.
  Names do not resolve yet, so give the ADDRESS in the URL and the NAME as
  the second argument; the server then serves the right site.
  Waking it puts a second driver on the microSD bus (the ESP32's GPIOs ARE
  the card's wires) -- measured harmless over 25 runs, but `koti-net off`
  when you are done is the right habit.

PEEKING
  `koti peek` reads the hardware directly, through /dev/mem.
    koti peek --list        everything worth looking at, by name
    koti peek mtime         the cycle counter, by name
    koti peek 0x40004       any physical address
  It refuses addresses whose registers change when you read them -- the
  keyboard and ESP32 receive queues would lose a byte to the peek.

WHAT IS NOT HERE
  NO package manager, no compiler, no floating point (koti has no FPU), and
  no manual pages -- this file is what there is. `busybox <applet> --help`
  covers the applets.

SHUTTING DOWN
  sync, then pull the power. `poweroff` and `halt` stop the CPU (the firmware
  halts on the SBI reset call); there is no power controller to switch off.

MORE
  docs/MANUAL.md in the repository, which this summarises.
EOF
}

# A heading is a line beginning at column 0 in capitals. Everything else is
# body text, which is always indented.
is_heading_re='^[A-Z][A-Z0-9 ()/-]*$'

case "${1:-}" in
	-l|--list)
		manual | grep -E "$is_heading_re"
		;;
	"")
		# ⚠️ `more` waits for a key at the end of the last screen. That is
		# right in front of a person and wrong in a pipe, so page only
		# when standard output is really a terminal.
		if [ -t 1 ]; then manual | more; else manual; fi
		;;
	*)
		want=$(echo "$1" | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]')
		out=$(manual | awk -v want="$want" -v hre="$is_heading_re" '
			$0 ~ hre { show = (index($0, want) == 1) }
			show { print }
		')
		if [ -z "$out" ]; then
			echo "koti-help: no section starting '$1'. Sections are:" >&2
			manual | grep -E "$is_heading_re" >&2
			exit 1
		fi
		printf '%s\n' "$out"
		;;
esac
